1. **A method for aqueous processing of cocoa beans**, the method comprising:  
   (a) combining whole or comminuted cocoa beans with water to form an aqueous cocoa-bean suspension;  
   (b) mechanically disrupting the cocoa beans in the presence of the water to release cocoa fat, cocoa solids, aroma compounds, and polyphenols;  
   (c) separating the aqueous cocoa-bean suspension into at least:  
   (i) a fat-enriched fraction,  
   (ii) a cocoa-solids-enriched fraction, and  
   (iii) an aqueous fraction comprising dissolved or dispersed aroma compounds and polyphenols;  
   (d) recovering cocoa butter from the fat-enriched fraction;  
   (e) drying and optionally milling the cocoa-solids-enriched fraction to produce cocoa powder;  
   (f) recovering at least one aroma compound from the aqueous fraction by distillation, evaporation, gas stripping, membrane separation, adsorption, or a combination thereof; and  
   (g) recovering a polyphenol-enriched composition from the aqueous fraction by membrane filtration, adsorption, solvent extraction, precipitation, concentration, drying, or a combination thereof,  
   wherein the cocoa butter, cocoa powder, recovered aroma compound, and polyphenol-enriched composition are obtained as separately recoverable products.

**1. A process for treating incubated cocoa beans and separately recovering cocoa butter, cocoa powder, cocoa aroma, and a polyphenolic powder, the process comprising:**

(a) providing incubated cocoa beans that have undergone microbial incubation and contain cocoa nib material, cocoa butter, volatile cocoa-aroma compounds, and cocoa polyphenols;

(b) heating the incubated cocoa beans under reduced pressure while maintaining the beans at a temperature of no more than 70°C, thereby removing moisture and generating a vapor stream comprising the volatile cocoa-aroma compounds;

(c) cooling the vapor stream to condense at least a portion of the volatile cocoa-aroma compounds, and separately collecting the resulting condensate as a cocoa-aroma fraction;

(d) dehulling the beans, before or after step (b), to obtain cocoa nibs;

(e) comminuting the cocoa nibs while maintaining the cocoa nibs at a temperature of no more than 70°C to form a cocoa liquor;

(f) mechanically pressing the cocoa liquor at a liquor temperature of no more than 70°C to separate:  
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(i) a liquid fat fraction comprising cocoa butter; and  
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(ii) a defatted cocoa press cake;

(g) clarifying the liquid fat fraction by sedimentation, filtration, centrifugation, or a combination thereof, without heating the liquid fat fraction above 70°C, and separately recovering the clarified liquid fat fraction as cocoa butter;

(h) dividing the defatted cocoa press cake into a first press-cake portion and a second press-cake portion;

(i) comminuting the first press-cake portion at a temperature of no more than 70°C to produce, and separately recovering, cocoa powder;

(j) contacting the second press-cake portion with a food-grade extraction solvent comprising water, a C1-C3 alcohol, or a mixture thereof, at an extraction temperature of no more than 70°C, thereby transferring at least a portion of the cocoa polyphenols from the second press-cake portion into the extraction solvent;

(k) separating spent cocoa solids from the extraction solvent to obtain a polyphenol-containing liquid extract;

(l) removing at least a portion of the extraction solvent from the polyphenol-containing liquid extract under reduced pressure while maintaining the liquid extract at a temperature of no more than 70°C; and

(m) drying the resulting concentrated polyphenol-containing extract at a product temperature of no more than 70°C to produce, and separately recovering, the polyphenolic powder;

**wherein each thermal treatment of the beans, nibs, cocoa liquor, cocoa butter, press cake, cocoa-aroma fraction, and polyphenol-containing extract is conducted such that the respective cocoa-derived material does not exceed 70°C, and wherein the cocoa butter, cocoa powder, cocoa-aroma fraction, and polyphenolic powder are recovered as four separate product streams.**
